## 4. Synthetic Network

Generate a fictional rural electricity distribution network containing:

- One transformer.
- One feeder.
- Six distribution sections.
- Ten households per section, giving 60 households.
- Six simulated candidate connection points, one per section.
- Transformer, section-level, household, and candidate-level measurement points.

Generate a full 24-hour day of data at 15-minute intervals, resulting in 96 intervals per meter per day.

Support the following scenarios:

A. Normal Day:
Household loads vary throughout the day. Simulate ordinary technical losses and small measurement errors. There should be no persistent unexplained anomaly.

B. Evening Unmetered Load:
Inject an additional simulated load between 7 PM and 10 PM at one hidden candidate connection. The default hidden location is U-03, within Section C.

C. Meter Fault:
Simulate one household meter under-recording its consumption. The detector should flag a discrepancy and consider a meter fault rather than automatically labelling it as theft.

D. Multiple Anomalies:
Simulate abnormal loads at two candidate locations.

E. Noisy Measurements:
Introduce missing readings and measurement noise. The application should identify low-quality data and reduce localization confidence.

Allow users to switch scenarios and regenerate data using a random seed. Re-running the same scenario with the same seed must reproduce the same measurements.

Keep the ground-truth hidden-load location separate from the detector's input. The localization algorithm must infer the location from simulated measurements rather than simply displaying the known scenario label.

